1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,650
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,709 --> 00:00:09,840
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:10,547
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,606 --> 00:00:13,139
Welcome to Unit 10: Light.

5
00:00:14,198 --> 00:00:20,268
We see things because of the light they give out or reflect. Why do soap bubbles shimmer with colours?

6
00:00:20,268 --> 00:00:25,985
Modern lamps use reflection and refraction to waste less energy. Let's explore how light behaves.

7
00:00:27,043 --> 00:00:31,699
Light is a small part of a huge family of waves, the electromagnetic spectrum,

8
00:00:31,699 --> 00:00:35,000
which also includes radio waves, microwaves and X-rays.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,714
The colour of light depends on its frequency: red is lowest, violet is highest.

10
00:00:39,714 --> 00:00:42,897
Mix all the colours together, and you get white light.

11
00:00:43,956 --> 00:00:50,203
Objects that give out light are luminous. The Sun is our main light source, powered by nuclear reactions.

12
00:00:50,203 --> 00:00:55,743
Lamps and LEDs turn electricity into light, fireflies glow by bioluminescence, and lightning,

13
00:00:55,743 --> 00:00:57,805
flames and lasers are luminous too.

14
00:00:58,864 --> 00:01:02,636
Most objects are non-luminous: they don't make their own light.

15
00:01:02,636 --> 00:01:09,001
We see them only when they reflect light from a source into our eyes. That's why you can't read in the dark!

16
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:16,543
Light comes in beams, made of many narrow rays. In a parallel beam, like a laser, the rays stay side by side.

17
00:01:16,543 --> 00:01:20,904
In a diverging beam, like a torch, they spread out. In a converging beam,

18
00:01:20,904 --> 00:01:25,501
like light through a magnifying glass, they meet at a point called the focus,

19
00:01:25,501 --> 00:01:27,858
which can be hot enough to start a fire!

20
00:01:28,917 --> 00:01:30,862
Light travels in straight lines.

21
00:01:30,862 --> 00:01:35,459
Look at a candle through three cards with holes in a row, and you can see it.

22
00:01:35,459 --> 00:01:38,642
Move one card out of line, and the candle disappears.

23
00:01:38,642 --> 00:01:43,238
Shadows form for the same reason: a solid object blocks light's straight path.

24
00:01:44,297 --> 00:01:48,776
Light is incredibly fast: almost 300 million metres per second in a vacuum.

25
00:01:48,776 --> 00:01:53,727
Light from the Moon reaches us in 1.28 seconds, and from the Sun in about 8 minutes.

26
00:01:54,786 --> 00:01:57,850
That's why we see lightning before we hear thunder.

27
00:01:57,850 --> 00:02:03,390
Distances to stars are measured in light years: a light year is about 9.5 trillion kilometres.

28
00:02:04,449 --> 00:02:06,571
Materials affect light differently.

29
00:02:06,571 --> 00:02:12,111
Transparent materials, like clear glass, let almost all light through, so we can see clearly.

30
00:02:12,111 --> 00:02:18,476
Translucent materials, like frosted glass and sunglasses, let some light through, but we can't see clearly.

31
00:02:18,476 --> 00:02:22,012
Opaque materials, like wood and metal, let no light through.

32
00:02:23,071 --> 00:02:29,436
When light hits a material, it can be absorbed, turning into heat; reflected, bouncing off; or transmitted,

33
00:02:29,436 --> 00:02:30,379
passing through.

34
00:02:31,438 --> 00:02:35,445
Dark colours absorb light well, which is why solar panels are dark.

35
00:02:35,445 --> 00:02:39,158
And unlike sound, light can travel through the vacuum of space.
